WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:05.750
Female narrator: The National Library of Medicine
is proud to present Desalination.

00:00:05.750 --> 00:00:07.060
A solution for our water needs.

00:00:07.060 --> 00:00:08.060
[recycle logo appears]

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:11.379
Female narrator: [female cartoon character
speaks] Did you know that 97% of Earth's water

00:00:11.379 --> 00:00:12.870
is saltwater from oceans?

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.339
[Cartoon boy appears.

00:00:14.339 --> 00:00:16.200
image of Earth appears with three red arrows
pointing at water in the oceans]

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.200
Cartoon boy: Hmm!

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:18.490
That's a lot of water!

00:00:18.490 --> 00:00:22.380
If there is so much ocean water, why don't
we just drink the water?

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:26.589
I don't get what everyone's saying about all
these water shortages and stuff.

00:00:26.589 --> 00:00:28.320
Female: It's not that simple.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:29.339
We can't drink saltwater.

00:00:29.339 --> 00:00:30.919
Cartoon boy: Why not?

00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:32.730
What would happen if we did drink it?

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:36.060
Female: Well, the salts in our food and drinks
ends up in our blood, (images of pizza, burger,

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:38.270
coffee, cake, and drink appear] but we can't
have too much salt in our blood.

00:00:38.270 --> 00:00:39.270
[cross-section kidney appears]

00:00:39.270 --> 00:00:43.650
Our kidneys use water to flush out the extra
salt, generating urine that exits the body.

00:00:43.650 --> 00:00:45.370
This is one of the reasons we need to drink
water.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:46.370
[human trunk, kidneys labeled]

00:00:46.370 --> 00:00:50.600
Cartoon boy: But if urine leaves the body,
then getting rid of salt must cause us to

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:52.970
lose water from our bodies!

00:00:52.970 --> 00:00:54.790
Female: That's correct!

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.010
And what is sea water?

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:59.890
Cartoon boy: I guess it's just water with
a lot of salt dissolved in it.

00:00:59.890 --> 00:01:00.890
Female: Exactly!

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:06.530
So, say, you drink one cup of sea water [cup
appears], and, say, in that cup there is a

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:08.861
tablespoon of salt [tablespoon of salt appears].

00:01:08.861 --> 00:01:12.420
The amount of salt in that cup of water is
so high, that it would take way more than

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:16.109
the one cup of liquid you drink to remove
the one tablespoon of salt from your body

00:01:16.109 --> 00:01:17.109
[many cups appear].

00:01:17.109 --> 00:01:20.479
Cartoon boy: So drinking sea water causes
you to lose even more water than you drink!

00:01:20.479 --> 00:01:24.570
Female: That's right, and losing too much
water causes dehydration, which is very bad

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:25.570
for you.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.140
Cartoon boy: It's such a shame, there's so
much salt water.

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.929
If we could only get rid of the salt, we wouldn't
have to worry about water shortages or anything

00:01:31.929 --> 00:01:32.929
like that.

00:01:32.929 --> 00:01:37.939
Female: Well, there is actually a way to remove
salt from sea water, it's called desalination,

00:01:37.939 --> 00:01:38.999
and it's more familiar than you think.

00:01:38.999 --> 00:01:39.999
[Flowchart of water cycle appears: precipitation,
evaporation, transpiration, condensation.]

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.310
Female: During the water cycle, water gets
desalinated.

00:01:43.310 --> 00:01:45.400
After rainfall the water flows toward the
ocean.

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:49.759
Energy from the sun causes the salty water
to evaporate, leaving the minerals on the

00:01:49.759 --> 00:01:50.759
ground.

00:01:50.759 --> 00:01:56.909
The pure water travels upward as a gas until
it hits cold air and condenses, forming clouds.

00:01:56.909 --> 00:01:58.869
Then fresh water falls as rain.

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:00.950
Cartoon boy: Hold on, condenses?

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:01.950
What's that?

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:03.149
Female: What is condensing?

00:02:03.149 --> 00:02:05.639
It's when water gas cools and turns into a
liquid.

00:02:05.639 --> 00:02:08.940
Have you ever drawn pictures on the bathroom
mirror after you took a shower?

00:02:08.940 --> 00:02:11.360
The water on the mirror came from your shower.

00:02:11.360 --> 00:02:15.450
When the water is hot, part of it turns into
gas, which then rises in the air.

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:19.250
When the gas comes into contact with a cold
mirror, it cools, or condenses, and turns

00:02:19.250 --> 00:02:20.250
into water.

00:02:20.250 --> 00:02:25.820
Again, clouds are just condensed water in
the air, and those clouds produce rain.

00:02:25.820 --> 00:02:29.590
Although nature produces fresh water, it is
a precious but limited resource.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:33.970
Desalinating ocean water provides much needed
freshwater for people to drink, especially

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:35.550
those in locations without much.

00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:37.040
Cartoon boy: So how do they do it?

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.750
Female: Well, there are two types of desalination.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:41.491
The first is called thermal desalination.

00:02:41.491 --> 00:02:44.330
[Illustration of thermal desalination appears.]

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:47.360
Thermal desalination is similar to the water
cycle.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:50.740
Saltwater is heated so that it evaporates,
leaving the salt behind.

00:02:50.740 --> 00:02:54.420
The evaporated steam is then cooled and condensed
into pure water.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.270
Cartoon boy: Like on the bathroom mirror!

00:02:57.270 --> 00:03:03.290
Female: Thermal desalination is a good method
because it works, and can handle large volumes

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:04.290
[picture of water silo].

00:03:04.290 --> 00:03:06.540
However, it does use up a lot of energy [picture
of lightning].

00:03:06.540 --> 00:03:12.360
Another method of desalination is membrane
desalination, which uses semi-permeable membranes.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:16.560
A semi-permeable membrane is a thin sheet
of material with tiny holes that let some

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:22.040
amount of liquids, such as water, but not
solids like salt, to pass through it.

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:27.310
To desalinate, membranes are used in a process
called reverse osmosis.

00:03:27.310 --> 00:03:30.460
This process involves tanks divided by a membrane.

00:03:30.460 --> 00:03:34.590
Pressure is applied to the saltwater side,
forcing water through the membrane from the

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:37.640
side where there is more salt, to where there
is no salt.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:44.290
Since the membrane only lets liquids in, the
salt gets left behind.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:49.550
Reverse osmosis is more efficient than thermal
desalination, meaning it produces more freshwater

00:03:49.550 --> 00:03:50.590
for less money.

00:03:50.590 --> 00:03:56.020
However, a reason not to use reverse osmosis
is that salt and other particles get stuck

00:03:56.020 --> 00:03:59.050
in the membrane holes, causing them to stop
working.

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.720
Membranes also get old and break down.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.510
Because of their short lifespan, membranes
must be replaced frequently, which is expensive.

00:04:06.510 --> 00:04:08.710
Cartoon boy: That makes sense.

00:04:08.710 --> 00:04:13.270
So if we have these technologies, why are
there still water shortages?

00:04:13.270 --> 00:04:18.120
Female: Industrial desalination is not yet
our main source of drinking water because

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.889
it requires a lot of energy, making it expensive
and harmful to the environment.

00:04:22.889 --> 00:04:26.870
Furthermore, harmful leftover salt is hard
to dispose of safely.

00:04:26.870 --> 00:04:32.169
However, scientists have started combining
technologies to make desalination more efficient,

00:04:32.169 --> 00:04:37.349
such as using both membrane and thermal desalination
technologies together and finding better ways

00:04:37.349 --> 00:04:38.879
to power desalination plants.

00:04:38.879 --> 00:04:43.270
Cartoon boy: So while we have a long way to
go, desalination really is a solution for

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:44.270
our water needs.

00:04:44.270 --> 00:04:44.272
The End.

